variables.content:
The Hofstadter problem — electrons in a two-dimensional lattice subject to a uniform magnetic field — is well understood when the underlying zero-field bands are topologically trivial: breaking of lattice translation symmetry at nonzero magnetic flux produces the fractal Hofstadter spectrum and magnetic-field-induced Chern bands. However, a systematic understanding was lacking for the inverse situation: inserting magnetic flux into a lattice whose zero-field electronic structure is already topological. Prior work typically treated (a) the Hofstadter spectrum of trivial Bloch bands, or (b) continuum/field-theoretic limits; there was no general, gauge- and lattice-aware framework for tight-binding models with arbitrary orbital positions and Peierls paths that (i) tracks how magnetic translation symmetry, flux periodicity, and embedding matrices act on the Bloch/Hofstadter Hamiltonian, and (ii) answers how established topological invariants (Chern number, Mirror Chern number, $Z_{2}$ Kane–Mele index, and fragile $w_{2}$) evolve as flux is threaded at fixed electronic filling. Concretely, before this work:
    - it was not settled whether threading an infinitesimal flux through a nonzero-Chern-number insulator at fixed filling must produce a bulk gap closing or whether the occupied-state manifold can remain continuous in flux; similarly, the fate of mirror-block Chern numbers under flux was not derived;
    - there was no general, model-independent statement relating the rational flux denominator $q$ (magnetic unit-cell enlargement) to the possible Chern numbers of gapped Hofstadter subspaces at the same filling as the zero-field insulator;
    - the classification of time-reversal-symmetric ($\mathcal{T}$) 2D topological insulators viewed as 3D $k_{z}$-parameterized Hamiltonians with $k_{z}\!\leftrightarrow\!\phi$ (flux) periodicity had not been linked to the flux-periodicity integer $n$ (where the Hofstadter Hamiltonian is periodic under $\phi\mapsto\phi+2\pi n$), leaving open whether the Hofstadter Hamiltonian realizes a strong 3D TI, a weak TI, or is trivial;
    - fragile topology (the second Stiefel–Whitney index $w_{2}$) and higher-order topology under flux were unexplored: it was unknown whether flux pumping can convert a fragile 2D phase into a 3D higher-order TI (HOTI) in the flux-parameter direction, and how that depends on microscopic Peierls paths;
    - numerical and analytic constructions of the Hofstadter Hamiltonian for arbitrary tight-binding models (arbitrary orbital positions, non-straight Peierls paths, and arbitrary commensurabilities) were scattered or incomplete, leaving ambiguous how to choose magnetic unit cells, embedding matrices, and gauge conventions systematically for proofs and numerics;
    - consequently, there was no unified set of rigorous theorems predicting which gap closings or topological pumpings are enforced by nonzero Chern numbers, Mirror Chern numbers, $Z_{2}$ invariants, or fragile $w_{2}$ under flux, nor clear guidance about the dependence of such statements on microscopic choices (Peierls paths) that are physically determined by Wannier overlaps.
    These gaps prevented a principled answer to whether new topological phases — especially phases impossible in crystalline insulators (e.g., discontinuous occupied manifolds at infinitesimal flux, Hofstadter semimetals, flux-pumped HOTIs) — must arise when flux is applied to a topological zero-field Hamiltonian, and impeded constructing model calculations and experimental proposals (notably for Moiré systems) that would access those regimes at laboratory magnetic fields.
